  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Probe-Station Bin Map",
    "overview": "The batch is read the way a yield engineer reads a lot: as a wafer bin map. Every unit is one square die cell laid on a serpentine probe path in time order inside a notched wafer outline, and state is carried by bin colour the way a sort map carries it. The world is polished-silicon grey with lighter panels, graphite ink and a graphite control rail, and hairline rules for structure. Nothing is decorative; colour appears only where it means a bin, an alarm or a drift state.\n\nDensity is engineer-grade: a readout spec table instead of KPI tiles, tabular figures, monospace for every measurement, unit ID and sensor name, and live counts in tab labels. The surface is flat. Depth comes from tonal layering (ground, panel, rail), never from drop shadows.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Wafer bin map as the primary reading of a batch: square cells, notched circular outline, shape plus colour legend with counts.",
      "Three-tone tonal stack: silicon ground, lighter panel, graphite rail.",
      "Bin colours (red fail, amber alarm/drift, pale green pass) are the only chromatic colour; graphite does all other work.",
      "Public Sans for interface text, JetBrains Mono only for measurements and identifiers.",
      "Flat surfaces, hairline borders, 6px panel corners, square data cells."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Bin Colour Rule",
        "body": "Chromatic colour means state. Red is a recorded failure, amber is an MSPC alarm or drift, pale green is pass or not queued. A colour that carries no bin meaning is graphite or a neutral.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Shape Plus Colour Rule",
        "body": "No state is shown by colour alone: missed failures are outlined cells, control-chart failures are triangles against circles, policy lines differ by dash, legends carry counts.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Mono Means Measured Rule",
        "body": "JetBrains Mono appears only on measurements, unit IDs and sensor names. Interface text, headings and labels stay in Public Sans.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Tabular Figures Rule",
        "body": "Any column or readout of numbers uses tabular numerals so values align across rows.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Tonal Stack Rule",
        "body": "Separate planes by tone and hairline, not by shadow: ground, panel, rail.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Do read any batch-level result on the wafer bin map before any table.",
      "Do reserve red, amber and pale green for bin state, and pair each state with a shape, dash or label.",
      "Do set measurements, unit IDs and sensor names in JetBrains Mono at 0.86em.",
      "Do use the readout table (label, tabular value, muted note) for headline numbers, with the random baseline next to every catch count.",
      "Do separate surfaces with tone and 1px hairline (`hairline`) borders."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "Don't add a KPI-tile grid; the readout table replaces it.",
      "Don't add drop shadows or elevation; the system is flat.",
      "Don't round die cells; data cells stay square.",
      "Don't introduce a chromatic accent that carries no bin meaning."
    ]
  }
}